.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_USED_COUNT 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_used_count \- report how many pool elements are in use
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_used_count(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of pool-owned elements currently handed out to
callers. Elements sitting in a per-thread cache have been taken off the
shared free list but not given to anyone, and are not counted as used.
Dynamic fallback allocations are not counted either; see
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) for those.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of elements currently in use
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Exact whenever the pool is quiescent. If other threads are allocating or
freeing while this runs, the result is a snapshot that may already be
stale by the time it is returned, which is inherent in asking for a count
of something that is moving.

On a quiescent pool the result never exceeds
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(). Under concurrent allocation on a pool with
per-thread caches it can briefly sit a few entries above it, and never
above the physical slot count that ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3)
accounts for; see ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) for why.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Acquires the pool's lock and walks its per-thread caches, so it is a
cold-path call and not intended for use inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
